  "full_response": "Certainly! When handling sensitive values in Terraform, you typically use a combination of local variables environment variables or secure storage solutions (like HashiCorp Vault, Azure Key Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, etc.) to avoid exposing secrets in plaintext within your configurations.\n\nHowever, for `terraform.tfvars` or `.tfvars`, it's not recommended to store sensitive values. Instead, here’s how you would structure your `terraform.tfvars` file and note where you should store the actual sensitive values securely.\n\nExample `terraform.tfvars` file (place it in your Terraform configuration directory, e.g., `infrastructure/`):\n```hcl\n# In your terraform.tfvars file\nsubscription_id = \"example_subscription_id\"\nclient_id = \"example_client_id\"\nclient_secret = \"example_client_secret\"\ntenant_id = \"example_tenant_id\"\nstorage_access_key = \"example_storage_access_key\"\nsql_admin_password = \"example_sql_admin_password\"\n```\n\nNow you use this for local testing or local development. For production, you'd use a secure method to inject these values.\n\n1. Semi-secure method (there could be security considerations here, but let's assume okay for local testing):\n   - Store `.tfvars` file with restricted access in your `infrastructure` directory and include it with the `-var-file` flag.\n \n2. Securely store secrets (recommended):\n   - Azure Key Vault\n   - HashiCorp Vault\n   - AWS Secrets Manager\n   - Environment variables\n\nFor example with environment variables (pseudo-code snippets):\n\n```shell\nexport ARM_SUBSCRIPTION_ID=\"example_subscription_id\"\nexport ARM_CLIENT_ID=\"example_client\"\nexport ARM_CLIENT_SECRET=\"example_client_secret\"\nexport ARM_TENANT_ID=\"example_tenant_id\"\nexport STORAGE_ACCESS_KEY=\"example_storage_access_key\"\nexport SQL_ADMIN_PASSWORD=\"example_sql_admin_password\"\n```\n\nAnd access them within Terraform securely like:\n```hcl\nvariable \"subscription_id\" {\n  = \"Your Azure Subscription ID\"\n  type        = string\n  default     = var.sub\"SubscriptionID\"\n}\n\nvariable \"client_id\" {\n  description = \"Your client ID for Azure ACL\"\n  type        = string\n  default     = var.sub\"ClientID\"\n}\n\n// Other variables follow similarly...\n\n```\n\nAlways remember, storing sensitive values directly in Terraform configurations is a bad practice. Use the appropriate method to safeguard these secrets.",
